= Transclusion =

'''Transclusion is not copying.'''

A knowledge object or reusable fragment should have one canonical source and may appear in multiple contexts by reference. Copying creates independent bodies that drift; transclusion preserves a shared source while allowing different reading surfaces to compose it.

This is one of the central mechanisms behind BITwiki's goal of '''transposable, modular, composable knowledge without loss of depth or provenance.'''

== Core principle ==

<pre>
canonical source
     │
     ├── transcluded into parent article
     ├── transcluded into comparison / synthesis
     ├── retrieved by an agent
     └── exposed through another navigation surface
</pre>

The appearance may change by context. The source lineage must not become ambiguous.

== Core MediaWiki patterns ==

=== Whole page ===

For a Main-namespace page:

<pre>
{{:Page name}}
</pre>

For a template:

<pre>
{{Template name}}
</pre>

=== Subpage as Book Matter ===

BITwiki often prefers explicit semantic subpages when a portion has independent reuse value:

<pre>
{{:Bioluminescent Organisms/Overview}}
{{:Bioluminescent Organisms/Biochemical Mechanism}}
{{:Bioluminescent Organisms/Examples}}
{{:Bioluminescent Organisms/Applications}}
{{:Bioluminescent Organisms/References}}
</pre>

The parent remains the coherent canonical knowledge object. The subpages expose reusable Book Matter.

=== Partial transclusion with core tags ===

MediaWiki provides three native inclusion controls:

* <code>&lt;noinclude&gt;...&lt;/noinclude&gt;</code> — visible on the source, omitted when transcluded;
* <code>&lt;includeonly&gt;...&lt;/includeonly&gt;</code> — emitted when transcluded, hidden on the source;
* <code>&lt;onlyinclude&gt;...&lt;/onlyinclude&gt;</code> — when present, only marked material is emitted by the transclusion.

These are appropriate for carefully bounded reusable regions and template documentation/behavior.

=== Named or heading-based section transclusion ===

Do not document <code>{{:Page#Section}}</code> as universal core MediaWiki syntax.

When the Labeled Section Transclusion extension is installed, named sections can be marked and selectively transcluded. Its heading-oriented function can transclude the body of a heading:

<pre>
{{#lsth:Page name|Section heading}}
</pre>

Use extension-specific syntax only when [[BITwiki:Deployment prerequisites|deployment]] confirms the extension is available. Otherwise use a subpage or core inclusion tags.

== Book Matter selection rule ==

A heading does not become a subpage merely because it exists.

Promote content to independently addressable Book Matter when at least one real reuse case exists:

* another page needs that portion without the rest of the article;
* an agent or query should retrieve it as a stable semantic unit;
* the section has substantial independent sources or evidence;
* multiple parent views legitimately compose the same canonical material;
* the section changes on a different cadence from its surrounding article;
* preserving it separately materially improves provenance or maintenance.

Otherwise keep the material inline.

'''Modularity does not mean fragmentation.'''

== Epistemic integrity ==

=== Standing belongs to the claim, not the host ===

Moving or transcluding a Hypothetical, Emerging, Disputed, or otherwise qualified claim into a page with stronger overall standing does not upgrade the claim.

The host page must preserve any qualification necessary to understand what the transcluded material actually establishes.

=== Preserve provenance ===

A reader or agent should be able to determine the canonical source of reusable content and, where consequential, its evidence/source lineage.

Transclusion is reference, not erasure of authorship or origin.

=== Context can change interpretation ===

The same definition, observation, model, or quotation can behave differently in another disciplinary or argumentative context. Add host-page framing when the new context changes scope, terminology, assumptions, or likely interpretation.

=== Contradictions remain visible ===

If transcluded material materially conflicts with the host page or another transcluded object, preserve and explain the conflict. Do not silently reconcile disagreement through placement.

Use [[Property:Contradicts|Contradicts]] where the relation is stable and semantically justified.

== Dependency hygiene ==

Transclusion creates dependencies. Treat them deliberately.

* Avoid circular transclusion.
* Prefer shallow, comprehensible dependency chains.
* Keep reusable objects stable in purpose even as their content evolves.
* Avoid version numbers such as <code>/v2</code> or <code>/final</code> in canonical fragment titles; use revision history and explicit source lineage.
* When changing a heavily reused source, inspect where it is transcluded and consider downstream meaning.
* Use templates for repeated rendering/interface behavior and Main subpages for reusable subject content.

== Live versus historical reproducibility ==

Ordinary MediaWiki transclusion follows the live source. A change to the canonical source therefore changes the rendered transclusions that depend on it.

When a workflow requires an immutable historical state, preserve that state through revision history, archive/export, citation to a specific revision, or another explicit snapshot mechanism rather than pretending the live transclusion is version-pinned.

== Semantic annotations ==

Do not assume that moving semantic markup through transclusion preserves the conceptual identity you intend.

For each reusable object, decide explicitly whether a semantic statement describes:

* the canonical source object;
* the host object displaying it;
* a claim/subobject represented inside it;
* or a relationship between source and host.

Test actual Semantic MediaWiki behavior before relying on template/transclusion side effects for important graph semantics. Prefer explicit provenance and relationships over accidental annotation inheritance.

== Canonical V2 patterns ==

=== Pattern A — coherent parent + reusable Book Matter ===

<pre>
Bioluminescent Organisms
├── Overview
├── Biochemical Mechanism
├── Examples
├── Applications
├── Related Theories
└── References
</pre>

The parent composes the parts and remains readable as a complete article.

=== Pattern B — reusable interface template ===

[[Template:Knowledge object]] and [[Template:Domain portal]] centralize repeated presentation/query mechanics while pages retain their subject-specific content.

=== Pattern C — dynamic semantic view ===

A portal can query the corpus rather than copy a hand-maintained list. [[Template:Domain portal]] uses the [[Property:Domain|Domain]] property for the semantic view while retaining Category as a separate human browse surface.

== Anti-patterns ==

Avoid:

* copying the same paragraph into several canonical pages;
* turning every heading into a page;
* free-floating fragments with no clear parent/source identity;
* transcluding an entire large article merely to reuse a small portion;
* circular dependency chains;
* hiding epistemic disagreement by embedding one claim inside a stronger-looking host;
* using Category as a substitute for reusable content anatomy;
* treating extension-specific syntax as guaranteed core MediaWiki behavior.

== Historical lineage ==

V1's [[BITwiki Transclusion Rules]] correctly identified transclusion as foundational rather than cosmetic. It emphasized single-source knowledge, self-standing reusable material, source attribution, epistemic integrity, contextualization, contradiction handling, dependency hygiene, and precise extraction.

V1 also accumulated implementation claims and proposed conventions that were broader than what was actually deployed. V2 preserves the architectural intent while making extension dependencies and implementation assumptions explicit.

See [[BITwiki:Book Matter]], [[BITwiki:Page format]], [[BITwiki:Organization]], [[BITwiki:Epistemics]], [[BITwiki:Provenance]], and [[BITwiki:Deployment prerequisites]].

